The Breakdown of "400"
Linux permissions use a three-digit octal code corresponding to three distinct user tiers: Owner, Group, and Others. [1, 2]
text
  chmod     4       0       0
            │       │       │
          Owner   Group   Others
         (Read)  (None)   (None)
Use code with caution.


Each digit represents the mathematical sum of the enabled permissions for that tier: [1, 2, 3, 4]
• Read (r) = 4
• Write (w) = 2
• Execute (x) = 1
• First Digit (4): The owner has a score of 4 (Read only). They cannot write to or execute the file.
• Second Digit (0): The group has a score of 0 (No permissions).
Third Digit (0): All other users have a score of 0 (No permissions)

